---
description: Prose sets running copy written as plain HTML, such as rendered Markdown or an article, with Carbon type styles, spacing, and line length.
---

<script>
  import {
    CodeSnippet,
    InlineNotification,
    Link,
    Prose,
    Text,
  } from "carbon-components-svelte";
</script>

## Basic

Wrap plain HTML in `Prose` to set it as running copy: paragraphs, headings, lists, links, inline `code`, code blocks, quotes, tables, rules, and figures. Each element gets a Carbon type style, the space between elements follows one rhythm, and lines stop at a readable length.

<Prose>
  <h3>Reporting API</h3>
  <p>The reporting API returns usage and billing data for every project in your organization, so finance and engineering read the same numbers. Send requests with an <code>Authorization</code> header, as described in the <a href="#basic">setup guide</a>.</p>
  <h4>Limits</h4>
  <p>Requests are limited per key:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>600 requests per minute, shared by every endpoint.</li>
    <li>13 months of history. <strong>Export</strong> anything you need to keep longer.
      <ul>
        <li>The export endpoint writes one file per day.</li>
      </ul>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <pre><code>curl -H "Authorization: Bearer $KEY" \
  https://api.example.com/v1/usage</code></pre>
  <blockquote>
    <p>Choose the region when you create the project. It can't be changed later.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Status</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>200</code></td><td>Success</td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>429</code></td><td>Rate limited. Retry after the number of seconds in <kbd>Retry-After</kbd>.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <hr />
  <p>Responses are JSON, paginated at 100 records, with a cursor for the next page.</p>
</Prose>

## Variants

Set `variant` to match the page the copy sits on. Each variant sets the body and heading type styles, the spacing between elements, and the line length. `condensed` shrinks the type but keeps compact's spacing and loosens caption-01's line height to 1.5, since 12px copy read line after line needs more room than a 12px label.

| Variant | Body | Headings | Line length | Use for |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `condensed` | caption-01 (12px), 1.5 line height | heading-01 and label-01 | 96 characters | Notes in inspectors, side panels, and other dense console UI |
| `default` | body-long-02 (16px) | Productive | 68 characters | Documentation, help, and other reading pages |
| `compact` | body-long-01 (14px) | Smaller productive, tighter spacing | 80 characters | Descriptions and help inside dense product UI, such as a side panel or modal |
| `spacious` | body-long-02 (16px) | Productive, one step larger, more room | 64 characters | Onboarding, getting-started guides, and other pages read once and slowly |
| `expressive` | body-long-02 (16px) | Expressive, scaling with the viewport | 60 characters | Editorial and marketing pages |

### Condensed

<Prose variant="condensed">
  <h3>Pod checkout-7f9c</h3>
  <p>Restarted 3 times in the last hour after failing its readiness probe. Memory peaked at 94% of its limit before each restart.</p>
  <h4>Events</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>OOMKilled at 14:02, 14:19, and 14:41</li>
    <li>Scaled from 4 to 6 replicas at 14:45</li>
  </ul>
</Prose>

### Compact

<Prose variant="compact">
  <h3>Retention policy</h3>
  <p>Logs are kept for 30 days, then deleted. Audit events are kept for a year and can't be deleted early.</p>
  <h4>Exceptions</h4>
  <ul>
    <li>Projects on a legal hold keep every log until the hold is lifted.</li>
    <li>Deleted projects lose their logs after 7 days.</li>
  </ul>
</Prose>

### Spacious

<Prose variant="spacious">
  <h3>Welcome to the console</h3>
  <p>Create a project to get an API key, then send your first request. It takes about five minutes.</p>
  <h4>Before you start</h4>
  <p>You need an organization admin to approve billing.</p>
</Prose>

### Expressive

<Prose variant="expressive">
  <h3>Designing for the people who use it most</h3>
  <p>We spent a month watching support agents work through their queues. Most of their time went to finding the customer's history, not answering the question.</p>
  <blockquote>
    <p>I know the answer in ten seconds. Finding the account takes two minutes.</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p>So the new console opens on the customer, not the ticket.</p>
</Prose>

## Max width

Each variant caps the line length so long lines stay easy to follow. Set `maxWidth` to a number of pixels or any CSS length to change it, or `"none"` to fill the container, for example in a narrow column that already limits the width.

<Prose maxWidth="none">
  <p>This paragraph fills the whole column. It's set with the default variant's type and spacing, but without the 68-character line length, so it runs as wide as the space it's placed in.</p>
</Prose>

## Offscreen code blocks

Set `deferOffscreen` on a page with many code blocks or figures. The browser then skips laying out and painting each `pre` and `figure` until it nears the viewport, with CSS `content-visibility: auto`. Each reserves 10rem until it first renders, then keeps its last rendered height. On a page of 60 code blocks, layout at load dropped from about 49 ms to 13 ms.

Skipped blocks stay in find in page, anchor navigation, and the accessibility tree. Text alone is cheap to lay out, so a page without much code gains little. Tables aren't skipped: the containment that skipping needs clips anything that overflows.

To scroll to an element inside a code block with script, read its position first, for example with `getBoundingClientRect()`. `scrollIntoView` on an element inside a block that is still skipped does nothing.

<Prose deferOffscreen>
  <p>Run the build, then serve the output:</p>
  <pre><code>npm run build
npx vite preview</code></pre>
</Prose>

## Carbon components inside

Prose doesn't style Carbon components or anything inside them, so components keep their own styles: a `Link` keeps its color, a `CodeSnippet` its background and padding, and `Text` its type style. That includes plain elements you pass into a component's slot, such as the link in this notification. Components still take part in the spacing between elements.

<Prose>
  <h3>Install</h3>
  <p>Add the package with your package manager:</p>
  <CodeSnippet code="npm i carbon-components-svelte" />
  <InlineNotification lowContrast hideCloseButton kind="info" title="Styles">
    <svelte:fragment slot="subtitleChildren">Import the CSS once, as the <a href="#carbon-components-inside">setup guide</a> shows.</svelte:fragment>
  </InlineNotification>
  <Text type="helper-text-01">Text keeps its own type style inside Prose.</Text>
  <p>Read the <Link href="#carbon-components-inside">theming guide</Link> next.</p>
</Prose>

## Rendered HTML

Prose styles markup it didn't render, such as HTML from a Markdown renderer or a CMS, set with `{@html}`. Only render HTML you trust or have sanitized.

<FileSource src="/framed/Prose/ProseRenderedHtml" />

## Wrapped sections

Children of a `section` or `article` inside Prose are spaced the same way as children of Prose itself, so you can wrap each section, for example to give it an `id` for a [TableOfContents](/components/TableOfContents).

<Prose>
  <section>
    <h3>Profile</h3>
    <p>Your name, photo, and email address appear wherever teammates mention or assign you.</p>
  </section>
  <section>
    <h3>Security</h3>
    <p>Change your password, turn on two-factor authentication, and review the devices signed in to your account.</p>
  </section>
</Prose>

## Nested

A Prose inside another sets its own copy: each element takes its type from the nearest Prose, so a compact block inside a default page reads at 14px, and its lists keep their bullets.

<Prose>
  <p>Requests are limited per key. When you hit the limit, the API returns status 429 with a header that says how long to wait.</p>
  <Prose variant="compact">
    <p>Compact Prose nested in the page's Prose, for a note or a sidebar:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Retry after the number of seconds in the header.</li>
      <li>Batch requests to stay under the limit.</li>
    </ul>
  </Prose>
  <p>Limits reset every minute.</p>
</Prose>

## Markdown output

Prose styles what Markdown renderers produce for GitHub-flavored Markdown, so rendered pages need no components.

### Aligned table columns

Cells keep the alignment renderers set with the `align` attribute for `:---:` and `---:` columns.

<Prose>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th>Region</th><th align="center">Status</th><th align="right">Requests</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td>us-east</td><td align="center">OK</td><td align="right">6,204,118</td></tr>
      <tr><td>ap-south</td><td align="center">Degraded</td><td align="right">2,109,872</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
</Prose>

### Heading anchors

Add an `a` with the `bx--prose__heading-anchor` class inside a heading to link to it. On devices that hover, it stays hidden until the heading is hovered or the link is focused. Give it an `aria-label` that names the section, such as "Link to Setup", since screen readers would otherwise read "#".

Elements with an `id` inside Prose take `scroll-margin-top` from the `--ccs-prose-scroll-margin` custom property. Set it to a sticky header's height so links to a heading land below the header.

<Prose>
  <h4 id="prose-anchor-setup">Setup <a class="bx--prose__heading-anchor" href="#prose-anchor-setup" aria-label="Link to Setup">#</a></h4>
  <p>Create a project in the console, then generate an API key.</p>
</Prose>

### Alerts

GitHub alerts (`> [!NOTE]`) render as a `blockquote` with `data-alert` set to `note`, `tip`, `important`, `warning`, or `caution`. They take InlineNotification's colors without its JavaScript. Set `data-alert-title` to show a title, in the reader's language.

<Prose>
  <blockquote data-alert="note" data-alert-title="Note"><p>Keys are scoped to one project.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="tip" data-alert-title="Tip"><p>Use one key per environment.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="important" data-alert-title="Important"><p>Regions can't be changed after you create a project.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="warning" data-alert-title="Warning"><p>Requests over the limit return <code>429</code>.</p></blockquote>
  <blockquote data-alert="caution" data-alert-title="Caution"><p>Revoking a key breaks every client that uses it.</p></blockquote>
</Prose>

### Task lists

Items with the `task-list-item` class, as GitHub and remark-gfm render task lists, show their checkbox in place of the bullet. Wrap each checkbox and its text in a `label`, so the checkbox has an accessible name.

<Prose>
  <ul class="contains-task-list">
    <li class="task-list-item"><label><input type="checkbox" disabled checked /> Create the project</label></li>
    <li class="task-list-item"><label><input type="checkbox" disabled /> Generate an API key</label></li>
  </ul>
</Prose>

### Highlighted code

Prose sets the padding, type, and surface of `pre`. Highlighters that mark tokens with classes, such as Prism, work as they are: load a Prism theme for the token colors. Shiki writes colors inline instead, so a light Shiki theme stays light in the g100 theme. Use Shiki's CSS variables theme and map its variables to Carbon tokens, so code follows the theme:

```js
import { createCssVariablesTheme, createHighlighter } from "shiki";

const carbon = createCssVariablesTheme({ name: "carbon" });
const highlighter = await createHighlighter({ themes: [carbon], langs: ["js"] });
const html = highlighter.codeToHtml(code, { lang: "js", theme: "carbon" });
```

```css
.bx--prose {
  --shiki-foreground: var(--cds-text-01);
  --shiki-background: var(--cds-ui-01);
  --shiki-token-comment: var(--cds-text-02);
  --shiki-token-keyword: var(--cds-link-01);
  --shiki-token-string: var(--cds-support-02);
  --shiki-token-constant: var(--cds-support-04);
  --shiki-token-function: var(--cds-text-01);
  --shiki-token-punctuation: var(--cds-text-02);
}
```

## Usage

| Do | Why |
| --- | --- |
| Write plain elements (`p`, `h2`, `ul`, `a`) inside Prose, not `Text` for each one. | Prose styles plain markup. `Text` and other Carbon components keep their own type styles, so they don't match the copy around them. |
| Use Prose for copy you didn't lay out element by element: rendered Markdown, CMS content, or long help text. | That's where styling each element with a component isn't possible or isn't worth it. For a few lines in a layout, `Text` gives finer control. |
| Use `compact` inside product UI and `default` on pages people read from top to bottom. | Product UI is set at 14px, so 16px copy inside a panel looks out of scale. |
| Set `maxWidth="none"` when the container already limits the width. | The line length cap would otherwise leave a gap beside the copy in a wide container. |
| Restyle an element with one class and its element, such as `p.lead`. | Prose's element rules weigh one class and one element, so a class alone ties or loses. |
| Put a Prose directly inside another Prose, or in a plain wrapper, rather than inside a Carbon component that sits in Prose. | Prose skips everything inside a Carbon component within it, and it can't tell a component's content from a Prose nested there, so Prose inside a Tile, Accordion, or Modal inside Prose renders unstyled. Telling them apart needs CSS `@scope`, which is newer than the browsers Carbon supports. |
